{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Gutless says the timing of a water preload should depend on your age because gastric emptying, which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach, tends to slow down as you get older. This means that a 30-minute water preload doesn't seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "A 30-minute water preload doesn’t seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals, which may be down to differences in the rate at which water leaves your stomach. As you get older, gastric emptying - which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach - tends to slow down."
    }
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The baseline TatarTTS models were built by randomly initializing VITS models for both female and male speakers. These models were then trained on the TatarTTS dataset for 1,000 epochs per speaker. The training process aimed to leverage the extensive data available for optimal model development.",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "Given the substantial volume of data within the TatarTTS dataset, exceeding 30 hours per speaker, we adopted a straightforward approach in training the TTS models. Our methodology involved the random initialization of VITS models for both female and male speakers. Subsequently, these models underwent training on the given dataset for 1,000 epochs per speaker."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Grapes play a significant role in Gutless, both in explaining energy density and in the author's diet. In the context of energy density, grapes are used as an example to illustrate how lower energy density foods can keep you feeling fuller for longer. In the author's diet, frozen grapes are used as a dessert substitute that satisfies the sweet tooth without causing overconsumption of calories.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "If you wanted to eat 150 calories of raisins, you’d be able to eat approximately 50 grams. Yet the same number of calories would give you almost 220 grams of grapes — more than four times as much."
    },
    {
      "ref": 3,
      "quote": "Frozen grapes have an energy density of just 0.7. This compares favorably to a tub of Ben & Jerry’s Peanut Butter Cup, which has an energy density of 3.4."
    }
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}
